Supreme People's Court

Notice on Issuing the Measures of the People's Courts on Withdrawal from Judge Posts (Trial)

Fa Fa [2020] No. 2

The High People's Courts of all provinces, autonomous regions, and municipalities directly under the Central Government; the Production and Construction Corps Branch of the High People's Court of the Xinjiang Uygur Autonomous Region:

The Measures of the People's Courts on Withdrawal from Judge Posts (Trial), having been studied and approved by the Supreme People's Court Party Group at its meeting, are hereby issued to you. Please promptly forward them to the Intermediate and Primary People's Courts within your jurisdictions, organize earnest study, and implement them in light of actual conditions. Any relevant circumstances and problems encountered in the work shall be reported level by level to the Supreme People's Court in a timely manner.

January 8, 2020

Measures of the People's Courts on Withdrawal from Judge Posts (Trial)

Article 1 These Measures are formulated in accordance with the Organic Law of the People's Courts, the Judges Law, and other relevant provisions, in line with the spirit of the Central Committee's advancement of judicial system reform, and in light of the actual work of the People's Courts, for the purpose of further improving the judge management system, continuously deepening the reform of the judge post system, and establishing a mechanism for the management of judge posts that allows for both entry and exit.

Article 2 Withdrawal from judge posts shall adhere to the following principles:

(1) Party management of cadres;

(2) matching the person to the post;

(3) performance orientation;

(4) ability to enter and exit;

(5) openness, fairness, and justice.

Article 3 Withdrawal of a judge from a post includes three types: voluntary withdrawal, automatic withdrawal, and mandatory withdrawal.

Article 4 A judge who voluntarily applies to withdraw from a post may, if there is a justifiable reason, withdraw from the judge post upon approval.

Article 5 A judge shall automatically withdraw from a post under any of the following circumstances:

(1) loss of nationality of the People's Republic of China;

(2) transfer out of the court where he or she serves;

(3) retirement or resignation;

(4) dismissal or expulsion according to law;

(5) adjustment to a non-judge post in the court as a result of serving exchanges.

Article 6 A judge shall withdraw from a post under any of the following circumstances:

(1) falling under the circumstances requiring withdrawal to avoid a conflict of interest;

(2) being unable to perform judge duties normally for more than one year due to health or other personal reasons;

(3) failing to meet the performance assessment standards for handling cases and being incompetent for the judge position;

(4) being unsuitable to continue serving as a judge due to disciplinary or legal violations;

(5) being required to withdraw from the post in accordance with the opinion of the Judges Disciplinary Committee;

(6) refusing to obey organizational arrangements to work in a judge post after being appointed to a quota;

(7) the spouse having moved to a country or region outside the mainland, or, if there is no spouse, all children having moved to a country or region outside the mainland;

(8) other circumstances where the person is unsuitable to hold a judge position.

Article 7 Where a judge is determined by the Judge Performance Evaluation Committee of the court where he or she serves to have any of the following circumstances, it shall be deemed to fall under "failing to meet the performance assessment standards for handling cases and being incompetent for the judge position" as specified in Article 6(3):

(1) the number, quality, and efficiency of cases handled fail to meet the prescribed requirements, and the ability to handle cases is clearly inadequate;

(2) due to gross negligence, serious quality problems occur in the cases handled, such as errors in evidence review, fact determination, or application of law, affecting fair adjudication and causing adverse effects;

(3) multiple problems arise in the quality and effect of case handling, and after comprehensive evaluation, the political and professional qualities do not meet the standards for a judge in a post;

(4) a judge responsible for trial supervision and management duties fails to perform duties earnestly in violation of regulations, causing serious consequences;

(5) other circumstances where the person is incompetent for the judge position.

Article 8 Where a judge in a post in a People's Court below the provincial level applies for withdrawal voluntarily or falls under any of the circumstances specified in Article 6, the organizational and personnel department of the court where he or she serves shall propose an opinion, which shall be studied by the Party Group of that court and then submitted level by level to the High People's Court for approval. The High People's Court shall complete the approval within two months; if the withdrawal is approved, it shall be reported to the provincial Judges Selection Committee for recordation.

Where a judge in a post in the Supreme People's Court applies for withdrawal voluntarily or falls under any of the circumstances specified in Article 6, the judge shall withdraw from the post upon approval by the Supreme People's Court Party Group, and the matter shall be reported to the Judges Selection Committee of the Supreme People's Court for recordation.

Article 9 Where a judge in a post in a People's Court below the provincial level falls under any of the circumstances specified in Article 5, the court where he or she serves shall submit the matter level by level to the High People's Court, which shall complete the withdrawal procedures within one month and report it to the provincial Judges Selection Committee for recordation.

Where a judge in a post in the Supreme People's Court falls under any of the circumstances specified in Article 5, the Supreme People's Court shall promptly complete the withdrawal procedures and report the matter to the Judges Selection Committee of the Supreme People's Court for recordation.

Article 10 A judge who objects to a decision concerning his or her own withdrawal from a post may, within seven days after receiving the decision, apply to the Party Group of the court where he or she serves for review.

Where the Party Group of an Intermediate or Primary People's Court, after review, does not change the original withdrawal decision, it shall, within thirty days from receipt of the judge's review application, provide a written reply to the judge concerned; where, after review, it intends to change the original withdrawal decision, it shall, within thirty days from receipt of the judge's review application, submit the matter level by level to the High People's Court for approval, then reply in writing to the judge concerned, and also report it to the provincial Judges Selection Committee for recordation.

The Party Groups of the High People's Courts and the Supreme People's Court shall make a decision within thirty days from receipt of a review application from a judge of the respective court and reply in writing to the judge concerned.

Article 11 Where a higher People's Court discovers that a judge in a lower People's Court falls under circumstances requiring withdrawal from a post but the lower court has not initiated the withdrawal procedure, the higher court shall urge the lower court to initiate the relevant procedure as soon as possible.

Article 12 Where removal of the legal position is necessary after a judge withdraws from a post, the relevant removal procedures shall be promptly initiated.

Article 13 Where a judge in a post falls under any of the circumstances specified in Article 5, the judge shall no longer exercise the powers of a judge in a post from the time such circumstances arise; where a judge applies for voluntary withdrawal from a judge post or falls under any of the circumstances specified in Article 6, the judge shall no longer exercise the powers of a judge in a post from the date the approving authority approves the withdrawal from the post.

Article 14 After withdrawing from a judge post, if a judge is transferred to a judicial administrative or judicial auxiliary position in the court, the court shall, in accordance with the authority for approving the promotion of judge grades, comprehensively consider such factors as the qualifications, work experience, and the trial performance, work performance, and withdrawal circumstances during the period as a judge in a post, and determine the post or grade by comparison within the prescribed quota range.

Article 15 A judge may reapply for a judge post five years after withdrawing from a post due to the opinion of the Judges Disciplinary Committee, or two years after withdrawing from a post due to the circumstances specified in Article 4 or Article 7. Where the conditions for entering the quota are met, the judge shall participate in examinations and assessments and be selected into the quota in accordance with the uniform procedures.

Article 16 These Measures apply to all judges in the national courts who are managed under the judge post system.

Article 17 Each High People's Court shall, in light of actual work, study and refine the circumstances and procedures for withdrawal from judge posts, and formulate specific implementation measures.

Article 18 These Measures shall be interpreted by the Political Department of the Supreme People's Court and shall come into effect on February 1, 2020.
